@page "/rides/{TrackId:guid}/edit"
@attribute [Authorize]
@using BlazorDLR.Shared.Components
@using BlazorDLR.Shared.Services
@using BlazorDLR.Shared.State
@using BlazorDLR.Shared.Tracks
@using DLR.Core.Contracts.Tracks
@using DLR.Core.Tracks
@inject IApiClient Api
@inject ConfirmService Confirm
@inject NavigationManager Nav

<PageTitle>Edit track — Dumb Luck Routes</PageTitle>

@*
	The track editor (§15.5), on every host.

	§6.1 and §13 Q15 originally made this web-only: trimming was judged a big-screen task
	and the phone got a note pointing at a desktop browser. That gate is gone — the screen
	a rider has with them at the end of a ride is the phone, and "open a laptop to cut the
	drive home off your track" is a worse answer than a cramped one.

	Four rules this page encodes:
	  1. Removals are half-open ranges of RAW indices (§15.5), not indices into the
		 simplified polyline the map draws. The raw-versus-simplified distinction is the
		 single most important implementation constraint in §15.5, and it is why the tap
		 lands on a point of the RAW list from GET /tracks/{id}/points even though what
		 the rider sees is the simplified line.
	  2. Nothing goes to the server until Apply. Trims accumulate in a TrackTrimSession,
		 undoable a step at a time back to the track as loaded — the rider can overshoot,
		 look at it, and walk it back without a round trip. A trim takes the cursor point
		 with it and leaves the cursor on the new edge, so holding a button chews along
		 the track instead of biting the same span twice; undo puts the cursor back where
		 it was as well as the points.
	  3. Apply is permanent. One request, and the client-side history is gone with the
		 session. The server keeps its own retained original (§15.6) and the API to reach
		 it still exists, but this page does not offer it: an undo button that appears
		 after the point of no return is an undo button that will be trusted at the wrong
		 moment. The confirm dialog is where that decision is made instead.
	  4. Distance/duration/ascent are recomputed by the server, not adjusted (§15.7). The
		 old and new numbers appear side by side after a commit — the number changing is
		 the whole point of trimming a lunch stop.
*@

<div class="page page-medium editor">
	<PageNav Title="Edit track" BackHref="@($"/rides/{TrackId}")" BackLabel="Back to the track" />

	@if (_session is null && _error is null)
	{
		<p class="empty">Loading…</p>
	}
	else if (_error is not null)
	{
		<p class="error">@_error</p>
	}
	else if (_session is { } session && _track is not null)
	{
		@if (_applied is { } applied)
		{
			<div class="result" role="status">
				<strong>Applied.</strong>
				Distance <strong>@FormatKm(applied.Track.DistanceM)</strong>
				(was @FormatKm(applied.PreviousDistanceM)).
				Points <strong>@applied.Track.PointCount</strong>
				(was @applied.PreviousPointCount).
				This cannot be undone from here.
			</div>
		}

		<p class="lead">
			Tap the track to put the cursor on a point, then trim away from it. Each trim takes
			the cursor point with it and leaves the cursor on the new edge, so pressing again
			carries on from where the last one stopped.
			<strong>@session.SurvivingIndices.Count</strong> of @session.Points.Count points remain.
		</p>

		@*
			The line under the cursor is the SIMPLIFIED shape of what survives — 43 000
			segments repainted on every pan of a long tour is not a map (§15.5). The cursor
			marker is drawn at the exact raw point it sits on, so what the rider is aiming
			at is never the simplified approximation.

			A removed span shows as a straight line joining its two sides. That is honest
			feedback about what was cut, and it is also where this drawing differs from the
			server's: an interior cut becomes a segment break there (§15.3), which one
			polyline overlay cannot express.
		*@
		<RideMap Camera="_mapCamera" Routes="_routes" Markers="_cursorLayer"
				 OnMapClicked="OnMapTapped" OnViewportReported="OnViewportReported" AllowRotation="false"/>

		@if (session.Cursor is { } cursor)
		{
			<div class="trim">
					<div class="cursor">Trim : Cursor on point <strong>@cursor</strong></div>
					<div>
						<span class="side-label m-0">◀ Back</span>
						<button type="button" class="primary" disabled="@_busy" @onclick="() => Trim(back: true, count: 10)">◀ 10</button>
						<button type="button" class="primary" disabled="@_busy" @onclick="() => Trim(back: true, count: 1)">◀ 1</button>

						<button type="button" class="primary" disabled="@(_busy || !session.HasEdits)"
								@onclick="Undo">
							Undo@(session.StepCount > 0 ? $" ({session.StepCount})" : "")
						</button>

						<button type="button" class="primary" disabled="@_busy" @onclick="() => Trim(back: false, count: 1)"> 1 ▶</button>
						<button type="button" class="primary" disabled="@_busy" @onclick="() => Trim(back: false, count: 10)"> 10 ▶</button>
						<span class="side-label m-0">Forward ▶</span>
				</div>		
			</div>
		}
		else
		{
			<p class="hint">Tap a point on the track to place the cursor.</p>
		}

		@if (_notice is not null)
		{
			<p class="error" role="alert">@_notice</p>
		}

		<div class="commit">
			<button type="button" class="danger" disabled="@(_busy || !session.HasEdits)"
					@onclick="ApplyAsync">
				@(_busy ? "Applying…" : $"Apply — remove {session.StruckCount} point(s)")
			</button>
		</div>

		<p class="warning">
			Nothing is sent until you hit Apply. Applying is permanent — the removed points
			are gone from the app, from exports and from every share link at once.
		</p>
	}
</div>

@code {
	[Parameter] public Guid TrackId { get; set; }

	/// <summary>How many points the map may draw before the line is simplified for display.</summary>
	private const int DisplayPointBudget = 2_000;

	/// <summary>
	/// The cursor pin's identity. Fixed, because the overlay upserts markers by id and the
	/// cursor is one marker that moves rather than a new marker per tap.
	/// </summary>
	private static readonly Guid CursorMarkerId = new("6b0d3c9e-1f5a-4a63-9d4a-3f2b7c5e1a80");

	private TrackDetail? _track;
	private TrackPointsResponse? _points;
	private TrackTrimSession? _session;
	private AppliedEdit? _applied;
	private string? _error;
	private string? _notice;
	private bool _busy;

	private MapCamera _mapCamera = new(0, 0, 2);
	private IReadOnlyList<RouteOverlay>? _routes;
	private IReadOnlyDictionary<Guid, MapMarker>? _cursorLayer;
	private MapViewport? _viewport;

	protected override async Task OnParametersSetAsync()
	{
		// A different track is a different edit. The banner and the error belong to the one
		// being navigated away from.
		_applied = null;
		_error = null;

		try
		{
			await LoadAsync();
		}
		catch (ApiException apiException) { _error = apiException.Error.Title; }
		catch (Exception exception) { _error = exception.Message; }
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Fetches the track and its full-resolution points, and opens a fresh session over them.
	/// Called again after a commit: the server has recomputed everything from the survivors
	/// (§15.7) and the version the next edit must quote moved with it.
	/// </summary>
	private async Task LoadAsync()
	{
		_track = await Api.GetTrackAsync(TrackId);
		_points = await Api.GetTrackPointsAsync(TrackId);

		// The wire carries coordinates only — this page never needs elevation or time, and
		// asking PolylineCodec for the points it encoded is the one decode implementation.
		_session = new TrackTrimSession(
		[
			.. PolylineCodec
			.DecodePoints(_points.Polyline)
			.Select(point => new TrackPoint(point.Latitude, point.Longitude)),
	]);

		// Frame on the bounding box, as the ride page does. A track with no bounds keeps the
		// whole-world camera rather than claiming a position off the Gulf of Guinea.
		if (_track.Bounds is { } bounds)
		{
			_mapCamera = new MapCamera(
				Latitude: (bounds.MinLatitude + bounds.MaxLatitude) / 2,
				Longitude: (bounds.MinLongitude + bounds.MaxLongitude) / 2,
				ZoomLevel: 11);
		}

		_notice = null;
		RefreshOverlay();
	}

	private void OnViewportReported(MapViewport viewport) => _viewport = viewport;

	/// <summary>
	/// A tap places the cursor on the nearest surviving point, or does nothing if it landed
	/// off the line. Doing nothing is deliberate: a tap on bare map that dragged the cursor to
	/// the far end of the ride would be a trim aimed somewhere the rider was not looking.
	/// </summary>
	private void OnMapTapped(MapClick click)
	{
		if (_session is not { } session || _viewport is not { } viewport)
			return;

		if (TrackHitTest.Nearest(viewport, session.SurvivingPoints, click) is not { } position)
		{
			_notice = "That tap missed the track. Tap closer to the line.";
			return;
		}

		session.PlaceCursor(session.SurvivingIndices[position]);
		_notice = null;
		RefreshOverlay();
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Takes a bite out of the track from the cursor, or sets a notice if the session refuses it.
	/// </summary>
	/// <param name="back">Whether the bite runs backwards along the track.</param>
	/// <param name="count">How many points to take, the cursor included.</param>
	private void Trim(bool back, int count)
	{
		if (_session is not { } session)
		{
			return;
		}

		TrimRefusal refusal = back ? session.TrimBack(count) : session.TrimForward(count);

		_notice = refusal switch
		{
			TrimRefusal.None => null,
			TrimRefusal.NoCursor => "Tap the track to place the cursor first.",
			// Qualified: this page's own generated class is also called TrackEditor.
			TrimRefusal.TooFewPointsRemain =>
				$"A track needs at least {DLR.Core.Tracks.TrackEditor.MinimumSurvivingPoints} points. "
				+ "To remove it entirely, delete the ride instead.",
			_ => null,
		};

		if (refusal == TrimRefusal.None)
		{
			// A commit's banner describes the track as it was before that commit. Once the
			// rider starts trimming again it is describing something else.
			_applied = null;
			RefreshOverlay();
		}
	}

	private void Undo()
	{
		if (_session is not { } session || !session.Undo())
			return;

		_notice = null;
		RefreshOverlay();
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Sends the accumulated removals as one edit. Confirmed first, because this is the step
	/// the client-side undo stack does not reach past (§15.5).
	/// </summary>
	private async Task ApplyAsync()
	{
		if (_busy || _session is not { HasEdits: true } session || _track is null || _points is null)
			return;

		bool go = await Confirm.AskAsync(
			$"Remove {session.StruckCount} point(s)?",
			"This is permanent. The removed points go from the app, from exports and from "
			+ "every share link at once, and cannot be undone from here.",
			confirmText: "Remove",
			danger: true);

		if (!go)
			return;

		_busy = true;
		_error = null;
		_notice = null;

		TrackSummary before = _track.Track;

		try
		{
			TrackEditResponse response = await Api.EditTrackAsync(TrackId, new EditTrackRequest(
				Version: _points.Version,
				Removals: session.Removals()));

			_applied = new AppliedEdit(response.Track, before.DistanceM, before.PointCount);

			// A fresh session over what the server now holds. The old history is deliberately
			// not carried across — there is no undoing past this point.
			await LoadAsync();
		}
		catch (ApiException apiException) { _error = apiException.Error.Title; }
		finally { _busy = false; }
	}

	/// <summary>Rebuilds the line and the cursor pin from the session's current state.</summary>
	private void RefreshOverlay()
	{
		if (_session is not { } session)
			return;

		IReadOnlyList<TrackPoint> line = DisplayLine(session.SurvivingPoints);

		_routes = line.Count > 1 ? [new RouteOverlay(PolylineCodec.EncodePoints(line))] : null;

		// MarkerKind.Rider is the overlay's bare disc-and-label rendering — no icon at all
		// (§16.3). That is exactly what a cursor wants: every curated icon key means something
		// about the world, and a hazard triangle sitting where the rider is about to cut would
		// read as a hazard.
		_cursorLayer = session.CursorPoint is { } point && session.Cursor is { } index
			? new Dictionary<Guid, MapMarker>
			{
				[CursorMarkerId] = new(
					Id: CursorMarkerId,
					Latitude: point.Latitude,
					Longitude: point.Longitude,
					IconKey: string.Empty,
					Title: $"#{index}",
					DirectionDeg: null,
					Kind: MarkerKind.Rider),
			}
			: null;
	}

	private static IReadOnlyList<TrackPoint> DisplayLine(IReadOnlyList<TrackPoint> points) =>
		points.Count <= DisplayPointBudget
			? points
			: TrackSimplifier.Simplify(new TrackGeometry(points)).Points;

	private static string FormatKm(double metres) => $"{metres / 1000:0.0} km";

	/// <summary>
	/// What a commit changed. The "was" figures are snapshotted at the moment of the request,
	/// because the page reloads the track straight afterwards and would otherwise quote the new
	/// numbers back as if they were the old ones.
	/// </summary>
	/// <param name="Track">The track as the server now holds it.</param>
	/// <param name="PreviousDistanceM">Distance before the commit.</param>
	/// <param name="PreviousPointCount">Point count before the commit.</param>
	private sealed record AppliedEdit(TrackSummary Track, double PreviousDistanceM, int PreviousPointCount);
}

<style>
	.editor .hint, .editor .warning {
		opacity: 0.75;
	}

	.editor .warning {
		margin-top: 0.75rem;
	}

	/* Cursor readout between the two trim sides, so "before" and "after" read as the two
			   directions away from the point named in the middle. */
	.editor .trim {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: space-between;
		gap: 0.75rem;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		margin: 0.75rem 0;
		padding: 0.75rem;
		background: #fff1;
		border-radius: 6px;
	}

		.editor .trim .side {
			display: flex;
			align-items: center;
			gap: 0.4rem;
		}

		.editor .trim .side-label {
			opacity: 0.75;
			font-size: 0.9rem;
		}

		.editor .trim .cursor {
			font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
		}

	.editor .commit {
		display: flex;
		gap: 0.5rem;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
	}

	.editor button {
		padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
		margin-top: 0.5rem;
	}

	.editor .result {
		margin: 1rem 0;
		padding: 1rem;
		background: #eff6ff20;
		border: 1px solid #bfdbfe;
		border-radius: 6px;
	}

	/*
				Narrow screens. This is what replaced the note that used to send a phone away: the
				composer stacks instead of hiding. Every control clears the 44 px touch target both
				platform guidelines publish, which the 0.5 rem padding above does not — and the trim
				buttons in particular are the ones a thumb will be jabbing repeatedly.
			*/
	@@media (max-width: 40rem) {
		.editor .trim {
			flex-direction: column;
			align-items: stretch;
		}

			.editor .trim .side {
				justify-content: space-between;
			}

				.editor .trim .side button {
					flex: 1;
					min-height: 2.75rem;
				}

			.editor .trim .cursor {
				text-align: center;
			}

		.editor .commit {
			flex-direction: column;
		}

			.editor .commit button {
				width: 100%;
				min-height: 2.75rem;
			}
	}
</style>
